Package: oneplus-erhai-erhaicam
Version: 2.9
Section: graphics
Priority: optional
Architecture: arm64
Maintainer: TheMojoMan <themojoman@gmx.net>
Provides: oneplus-erhai-cam
Replaces: oneplus-erhai-cam (<< 1.3)
Breaks: oneplus-erhai-cam (<< 1.3)
Depends: libgtk-4-1, libgdk-pixbuf-2.0-0, gstreamer1.0-libcamera, gstreamer1.0-gtk4, gstreamer1.0-plugins-base, gstreamer1.0-plugins-good, gstreamer1.0-plugins-bad, gstreamer1.0-pipewire
Recommends: iio-sensor-proxy, python3-numpy
Description: ErhaiCam — standalone camera app for the OnePlus Pad 3 (erhai)
 GTK4/GStreamer camera app with NPU-driven autofocus and auto-exposure.  The
 per-frame image analysis (Tenengrad focus metric + luminance statistics) runs
 on the Hexagon NPU (HTP) as an ONNX graph executed by ONNX Runtime's QNN
 execution provider (erhai-npu-analyze.py), with a CPU implementation of the
 same metric as the automatic fallback.  The AF sweep drives the kernel
 cn3927 lens subdev (V4L2_CID_FOCUS_ABSOLUTE, tap-to-focus, refocus on
 sharpness drop); AE is a log-domain controller on the SC1320CS integration
 time written straight to the sensor register 0x3e00 (BOTH cameras since 2.8 —
 the rear SC1320CS and the front SC820CS).  AE (top left) and AF (top right)
 buttons toggle the automatics; turning one off reveals the matching fader
 below the button for manual focus (VCM, 0..1023) or exposure (sensor lines,
 1..6396 rear / 1..3120 front) — the faders stretch with the window and stop
 50 px above its bottom edge.
 .
 2.9 MAKES LOW LIGHT USABLE — a frame-length (VTS) ladder in the AE.  A
 sensor's integration time can never exceed its FRAME LENGTH, and the mode table
 fixes that at 3128 lines (front SC820CS) / 6404 (rear SC1320CS): so in a dim
 room the front camera was simply out of light (measured: luma 43, signal band
 18 of 255) and NO setting of the software ISP could help (its AGC is inert on
 these sensors — libcamera has no CameraSensorHelper for either — and every
 option set measured identical).  Lengthening the frame is the one lever that
 works: `0x320e/0x320f` 3128 -> 6240 gave luma 43.4 -> 48.7 and band 17.9 ->
 24.3 (real light, contrast intact) for a 7.5 fps preview instead of 15.  The AE
 now doubles the frame (up to 4x) when the exposure is at its ceiling and the
 scene is still dark, moves the exposure to the new ceiling, and halves it back
 towards the mode table's length once the scene brightens — so a bright room
 keeps full frame rate.  `--no-ae` and manual exposure never touch the frame.
 .
 2.8 OWNED THE CAMERAS' EXPOSURE REGISTERS; the ISP tuning below follows.  Measured on two photos taken 13 s
 apart: the picture's black floor rose 10 -> 42 (out of 255) while the
 highlights barely moved and its colour temperature was unchanged — a fog, not
 a colour cast.  Cause: this pad's software ISP has NO exposure model for
 either sensor (libcamera: "Failed to create camera sensor helper") and the
 kernel's exposure/gain controls are no-ops on both, so in a dark scene the
 ISP's AGC could only brighten the picture in SOFTWARE, and its digital gain
 multiplies the black level until the frame washes out.  The app now owns BOTH
 sensors' exposure registers (so the ISP's AE cannot fight it and the front is
 no longer left entirely to it — the front has no AE of its own), and
 libcamera's gamma and saturation are pinned by default so its luminance-
 dependent `Adjust` compensation cannot re-curve the picture (gamma 2.2,
 saturation 1.0 = the pinned default).  Escape hatches: ERHAI_CAM_ISP=none
 restores the old behaviour (or `=raw`/`=off`/`=isp`), `=default`/`=on`/`=pins`
 is the default, `=lowlight` adds `ae-enable=false` (no ISP gain stage at all —
 darkest but never washed out), and any `key=value,…` is a complete override;
 ~/.config/erhai/erhaicam.conf `[isp] key=value` lines are appended to the
 default.
 .
 Also: photo capture into ~/Pictures/ErhaiCam, video recording, front/rear
 camera switching (front streams once its Phase-B driver work lands) and
 accelerometer orientation handling (upright preview + upright snapshots).
 .
 Video recording (1.6) is H.264 + AAC in MP4 through the hardware encoder
 (v4l2h264enc / Iris, which only takes NV12 — the previous I420 request failed
 its link and silently fell back to software VP8), with a VP8/Opus WebM
 fallback.  The note button at the bottom right of the shutter bar picks the
 soundtrack: "No audio" (silent video, the default) or "Microphone" — the
 PipeWire default source (the pad's Internal Mics), captured as a second branch
 of the same pipeline so the muxer keeps picture and sound in sync.  The
 choice applies from the next recording on.
 .
 RESOLUTION (2.4, default changed in 2.5): the button in the bottom-LEFT corner
 shows the stream size and opens a menu to choose it.  One stream feeds preview,
 video and photos, so the choice sets all three.  Each camera has its OWN list
 and its own remembered choice — switching camera switches the menu.  Default
 for BOTH cameras: 1920x1080 (Full HD).
   back  — 4152x3120 (sensor native), 3840x2160 (4K UHD), 3200x2400 (8 MP),
           2560x1920, 1920x1080 (Full HD, the default), 1280x720, 640x480
   front — 3200x2400 (8 MP), 2560x1920, 1920x1080 (Full HD, the default),
           1600x1200, 1280x960, 640x480
 (the front sensor's bare native 3264x2448 does NOT negotiate on this hardware;
 3200x2400 is its largest usable size).  The size is asked of the software ISP,
 so it is scaled before the colour conversion.  Both choices persist in
 ~/.config/erhai/erhaicam.conf, and the picker is locked while a recording runs
 (a new stream would end the take).  A scripted run can force a size with
 --res WxH or ERHAI_CAM_RES=WxH.
 .
 1.7 fixes the resulting timestamps: the camera's frames carry the PREVIEW
 pipeline's time base while the microphone starts at its own zero, so a
 recording made a while after launch came out with a lead-in of nothing (black
 picture, wrong duration) before the video began.  Each recording now re-bases
 its frames onto the first one recorded, so picture and sound start together.
 .
 PRIVACY: the microphone is opened ONLY while a recording runs (from pressing
 Record until Stop, or a headless --record run) and is closed again afterwards.
 No capture stream is ever left running in the background, and the cold start
 of the pad's capture path is handled by trimming the silent head of the track
 instead of by keeping the mic awake (owner decision, 2026-10-20).
 .
 This package takes over the renamed oneplus-erhai-cam (<= 1.2, the same app):
 it Provides that name, and Replaces/Breaks the old versions so an installed
 oneplus-erhai-cam upgrades here and its files (usr/bin/erhai-cam, the desktop
 file, the shipped source) change hands cleanly.  The oneplus-erhai-cam 1.3
 package is an empty transitional package that Depends on this one.
 .
 The manual-focus/exposure GNOME Shell extension (erhai-cam-focus@erhai.local)
 is retired: the app owns both loops natively now (oneplus-erhai-camera 1.3
 no longer ships it).
